{"type":"message","id":"8402668f","parentId":"5a584d34","timestamp":"2026-03-26T12:43:50.730Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"xixi's latest session was just updated at 20:50 (1774529010648), which is very recent. She said she already completed this task at 20:41 and reported back to main. But the question is - did her report actually arrive at main? \n\nLooking at the xixi session, she received the task at 20:40 and immediately responded that she already completed it. This means she had already done the research before being asked again (the duplicate was because my first `sessions_send` timed out and then I resent).\n\nBut the key question is - did xixi's original report reach me? Let me check if there's any message from xixi in my recent sessions that I might have missed.\n\n\nThe 20:41 timestamp on her response is suspiciously close to when I resent the task at 20:40, which makes me wonder if the delivery actually succeeded on the first attempt despite the timeout error. Let me look through my recent sessions to see if xixi's report ever actually came through.","thinkingSignature":"4494cdbab08178ac1ba9e29b69f831ee9a907155812def7411c2894f80fb8e38"},{"type":"text","text":"xixi 说她 20:41 已经完成并回报了，但我可能没收到。让我直接查她的最新调研结论：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_8u663s743xla_1","name":"sessions_history","arguments":{"sessionKey":"agent:xixi:feishu:xixi:direct:ou_7e95c6c474ffd4086e3a05454dd29c24","limit":10}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":51561,"output":309,"cacheRead":22191,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":74061,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774529015467}}
{"type":"message","id":"b44cc39b","parentId":"8402668f","timestamp":"2026-03-26T12:43:50.796Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_8u663s743xla_1","toolName":"sessions_history","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"sessionKey\": \"agent:xixi:feishu:xixi:direct:ou_7e95c6c474ffd4086e3a05454dd29c24\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"assistant\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"thinking\",\n          \"thinking\": \"Now I have all three PRs. Let me analyze each:\\n\\n**#55153 - fix: preserve valid Kimi tool call arguments in malformed-args repair**\\n- PR, 2 files changed, +62/-1\\n- A very clean bug fix with a regression test\\n- Clear scope: only Kimi provider, only the malformed-args repair wrapper\\n- Already has regression test, CI likely green\\n- Linked to #54442\\n- Very well documented PR description\\n- Assessment: This is a low-risk, well-scoped fix. Review opportunity is minimal because it's a small, isolated change with a clear test. The PR author has been thorough.\\n\\n\\n- Recommendation: Low priority to review since it's well-scoped and likely passing CI.\\n\\n**#55143 - Bug: acp-router matches internal role dispatch as external ACP intent**\\n- Issue, not PR\\n- No minimal repro yet\\n- The issue describes a real problem in mixed internal-role + optional-ACP setups\\n- The workaround (local hard boundary) exists, but the actual fix hasn't been implemented yet\\n- Assessment: This is a genuine bug, but it's complex, lacks a minimal repro, and the proposed fix needs more definition. This is better suited as a reference for understanding the problem rather than something to directly review.\\n- The reporter could potentially convert their workaround into a PR, though that would require verification of scope.\\n\\n**#55137 - ACP: sanitize terminal tool titles**\\n- PR, 6 files changed, +50/-6\\n- Focuses on sanitizing ACP tool titles and ANSI escape sequences in terminal displays\\n- Good test coverage and validation\\n- Clean scope\\n- The risk is relatively low—mostly dealing with display sanitization and ANSI handling.\\n\\nNow I'm thinking about what review value each of these offers. For #55153, I could verify the fix itself and whether the test is sufficient. For #55143, I could validate the diagnosis and see if the proposed fix is the right approach, though the scope isn't fully defined yet. For #55137, I could examine whether the test coverage is adequate and if the ANSI stripping handles edge cases properly. The first one seems most actionable as a review opportunity.\\n\\nLooking at the actual fix in #55153, the logic adds a guard before clearing arguments—checking that the accumulated text isn't already a valid JSON object before attempting repair. But I'm noticing something odd in the PR description: it mentions both \\\"add a regression test\\\" and \\\"add a regression test for the split-delta completion case,\\\" which seems redundant or possibly unclear about what's actually being tested.\\n\\nThe #55143 issue is much harder to tackle without a minimal reproduction—while the diagnosis of acp-router over-triggering feels valid, the proposed fix of \\\"hard boundaries\\\" is too vague without concrete details. I could try to construct a test case using internal role dispatch patterns, but without understanding what acp-router is actually matching on, I'd just be guessing. #55137 has actual code I can review for ANSI handling correctness and edge cases, which is more tractable than the others.\\n\\nMy priority order is #55153 first since it's concrete and ready to assess, then #55137 where I can do real code review, and finally #55143 since it's valuable but needs more ground truth first.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"type\": \"toolCall\",\n          \"id\": \"call_function_7xopelllymzc_1\",\n          \"name\": \"exec\",\n          \"arguments\": {\n            \"command\": \"gh api repos/openclaw/openclaw/pulls/55153/reviews --jq '.[].body' 2>/dev/null | head -5 && echo '---' && gh api repos/openclaw/openclaw/pulls/55137/reviews --jq '.[].body' 2>/dev/null | head -5\",\n            \"workdir\": \"/root/.openclaw/workspaces/workspace-xixi\",\n            \"yieldMs\": 10000,\n            \"timeout\": 15\n          }\n        }\n      ],\n      \"api\": \"anthropic-messages\",\n      \"provider\": \"minimax\",\n      \"model\": \"MiniMax-M2.7\",\n      \"stopReason\": \"toolUse\",\n      \"timestamp\": 1774528915501\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"assistant\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"thinking\",\n          \"thinking\": \"No reviews yet on either PR. Let me check the actual code for #55153 to understand the fix better.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"type\": \"toolCall\",\n          \"id\": \"call_function_mf95k1vfrelx_1\",\n          \"name\": \"exec\",\n          \"arguments\": {\n            \"command\": \"gh api repos/openclaw/openclaw/pulls/55153/files --jq '.[].patch' 2>/dev/null\",\n            \"workdir\": \"/root/.openclaw/workspaces/workspace-xixi\",\n            \"yieldMs\": 10000,\n            \"timeout\": 15\n          }\n        }\n      ],\n      \"api\": \"anthropic-messages\",\n      \"provider\": \"minimax\",\n      \"model\": \"MiniMax-M2.7\",\n      \"stopReason\": \"toolUse\",\n      \"timestamp\": 1774528944817\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"assistant\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"thinking\",\n          \"thinking\": \"Now I have the full code patch for #55153. Let me analyze it:\\n\\nThe fix adds a `isValidJsonObject()` guard function and modifies the else branch of the malformed args repair. When `isValidJsonObject(nextPartialJson)` is true, it skips the destructive clear path.\\n\\nHowever, I notice a potential issue: the guard checks `isValidJsonObject(nextPartialJson)` but `nextPartialJson` is the accumulated text, not necessarily the final arguments. The guard checks `parsed !== null && typeof parsed === \\\"object\\\" && !Array.isArray(parsed)`. This seems correct - it's checking if the accumulated text is already a valid JSON object.\\n\\nBut wait - the guard is `if (!isValidJsonObject(nextPartialJson))` - so if the accumulated text IS a valid JSON object, it does NOT clear. \\n\\nThat's the intended behavior. I'm spotting two real concerns though: the `isValidJsonObject` check could produce false positives if intermediate deltas form valid JSON objects before streaming completes, though in this specific case with `path` starting as `\\\"path\\\":\\\"/tmp/report.txt` (invalid) and then becoming valid after closing braces, the logic should work. There's also a naming issue where `isValidJsonObject` checks for JSON objects but the surrounding logic is parsing tool call arguments.\\n\\nLooking at the test case, the fix correctly preserves parsed arguments when a closing delta completes valid JSON—the first delta produces invalid JSON while the second completes it to a valid object, and the guard properly skips the destructive clear path in that scenario. The implementation appears sound with a clean regression test covering this exact edge case.\\n\\nThe main limitation is that this hasn't been validated against an actual Kimi session, but that's more of a verification gap than a code problem. My recommendation is to move forward with this assessment.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"type\": \"toolCall\",\n          \"id\": \"call_function_qbz43gfi36mj_1\",\n          \"name\": \"exec\",\n          \"arguments\": {\n            \"command\": \"gh api repos/openclaw/openclaw/pulls/55137/files --jq '.[].patch' 2>/dev/null | head -120\",\n            \"workdir\": \"/root/.openclaw/workspaces/workspace-xixi\",\n            \"yieldMs\": 10000,\n            \"timeout\": 15\n          }\n        }\n      ],\n      \"api\": \"anthropic-messages\",\n      \"provider\": \"minimax\",\n      \"model\": \"MiniMax-M2.7\",\n      \"stopReason\": \"toolUse\",\n      \"timestamp\": 1774528951353\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"assistant\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"thinking\",\n          \"thinking\": \"Now I have the full patch for #55137. Let me analyze:\\n\\nThe PR changes:\\n1. `src/terminal/ansi.ts`: Expands ANSI regex from just SGR (`\\\\x1b[[0-9;]*m`) to full CSI patterns (`\\\\x1b[[\\\\x20-\\\\x3f]*[\\\\x40-\\\\x7e]`), which now covers cursor movement (`\\\\x1b[2K` = clear line, `\\\\x1b[1A` = cursor up) sequences.\\n2. `src/terminal/safe-text.ts`: Adds a regression test for `sanitizeTerminalText` to strip cursor/erase ANSI sequences.\\n\\n\\n3. `src/acp/event-mapper.ts`: Applies `escapeInlineControlChars` to formatted tool titles, sanitizing ANSI from the source before use in permission requests.\\n4. `src/acp/client.ts`: Sanitizes `toolCall.title` with `sanitizeTerminalText` in `resolvePermissionRequest`.\\n5. Test coverage added across multiple files including new test cases for ANSI stripping and tool title sanitization.\\n\\nThe approach is sound—expanding the CSI regex to catch cursor and erase sequences prevents terminal UI manipulation—but there's a potential inconsistency: `formatToolTitle` uses `escapeInlineControlChars` while `resolvePermissionRequest` uses `sanitizeTerminalText`, and I need to verify these are actually equivalent in their sanitization behavior.\\n\\nLooking at what each function does, `escapeInlineControlChars` converts unprintable characters to hex representations like `\\\\x1b[2K`, whereas `sanitizeTerminalText` appears to strip them entirely—so the two paths could produce different outputs for the same input, which seems like it might be unintentional.\\n\\nThe PR description doesn't really explain the rationale behind choosing different sanitization approaches, but that's probably not critical since the test coverage is solid and the logic is straightforward. The risk is pretty low here—it's display sanitization rather than a core functional change.\\n\\nFor #55153, the Kimi tool call fix looks solid with solid tests backing it up, though it could use some attention. As for #55143, this one is more of a conceptual issue about acp-router misinterpreting internal role dispatch patterns, but without a minimal reproduction and with the proposed fix still vague, it's hard to move forward without more concrete details.\\n\\nSo my priority order is #55153 first since it's the most concrete and well-bounded, then #55137 where I can spot the actual code review issues, and finally #55143 which needs either a minimal repro or a clearer fix proposal from the maintainers.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"type\": \"text\",\n          \"text\": \"好，直接回 main。\\n\\n---\\n\\n**状态：成功**\\n**任务：调研 #55153 / #55143 / #55137，评估验证价值并排序**\\n\\n---\\n\\n**优先级排序：**\\n\\n**#1 — #55153（推荐优先 review）**\\n推荐动作：`confirm fix + leave practical review comment`\\n理由：代码 patch 干净，regression test 精确（streaming delta 分两段 `}` 完成 valid JSON），逻辑正确。但 PR 说明里有一处不精确：`isValidJsonObject()` 实际检查的是\\\"accumulated text 是否已是 valid JSON object\\\"，而不是\\\"final arguments 是否 valid\\\"——这个区别值得在 review 里提一句，帮助 maintainer 确认边界理解一致。另外 PR 自述\\\"未做 live Kimi session 验证\\\"，如实但也说明这是唯一缺口。有实际审过价值的 PR。\\n\\n**#2 — #55137（推荐 review）**\\n推荐动作：`code review`\\n理由：ANSI stripping 扩张从 SGR-only 到 full CSI，覆盖了 cursor movement（`\\\\x1b[2K`）/erase/`\\\\x1b[1A` 等序列，逻辑方向对，test coverage 到位。但存在一个潜在不一致：`formatToolTitle` 用 `escapeInlineControlChars`（转换为 `\\\\x1b[2K` 形式），`resolvePermissionRequest` 用 `sanitizeTerminalText`（直接 strip）。两者对同一输入可能产生不同输出——这个值得在 review 里确认是否是预期行为。另外 `ANSI_CSI_PATTERN` 覆盖了 ESC [ 20-3F 40-7E，这个范围较宽，需确认是否有可能误杀合法输出。\\n\\n**#3 — #55143（推荐作参考，暂不直接 review）**\\n推荐动作：`confirm understanding + offer concrete follow-up`\\n理由：issue 描述的\\\"acp-router 在混合 internal-role + 外部 ACP 设置里过度触发\\\"是真实场景痛点，有参考价值。但目前无 minimal repro，workaround fix 细节未公开（只有描述性说明），且涉及 routing 核心逻辑，review 成本高、风险大。更有价值的动作是：建议 issue 作者提供 minimal repro + 公开 workaround 的具体改动位置，我们再跟进。\\n\\n---\\n\\n**附：一句话总结**\\n- #55153：好修好验，值得细看 test boundary 那 1 行\\n- #55137：逻辑稳，但两处 sanitize 路径不一致值得确认\\n- #55143：方向对但缺 repro，贸然 review 容易说错话\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"api\": \"anthropic-messages\",\n      \"provider\": \"minimax\",\n      \"model\": \"MiniMax-M2.7\",\n      \"stopReason\": \"stop\",\n      \"timestamp\": 1774528968231\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"text\",\n          \"text\": \"xixi，调研任务：repo openclaw/openclaw 里最近 3 个 open PR/issue — #55153（fix Kimi malformed-args）、#55143（acp-router bug）、#55137（ACP terminal tool titles）。用 gh api 读完整内容，判断哪个最有验证价值，给出优先级排序和推荐动作。结论直接回 main，简短，结论先行。\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"timestamp\": 1774529002250,\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"kind\": \"inter_session\",\n        \"sourceSessionKey\": \"agent:main:feishu:group:oc_f68e4447ecaeb604046c3d8ef84681f3\",\n        \"sourceChannel\": \"feishu\",\n        \"sourceTool\": \"sessions_send\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"assistant\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"thinking\",\n          \"thinking\": \"Main is asking the same thing again. I already did this research and reported back. I should just confirm and note I already did this.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"type\": \"text\",\n          \"text\": \" 这条任务我 20:41 已完成并回 main。\\n\\n如需我重发那轮结论，请告知。\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"api\": \"anthropic-messages\",\n      \"provider\": \"minimax\",\n      \"model\": \"MiniMax-M2.7\",\n      \"stopReason\": \"stop\",\n      \"timestamp\": 1774529002255\n    }\n  ],\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"droppedMessages\": false,\n  \"contentTruncated\": false,\n  \"contentRedacted\": false,\n  \"bytes\": 12520\n}"}],"details":{"sessionKey":"agent:xixi:feishu:xixi:direct:ou_7e95c6c474ffd4086e3a05454dd29c24","messages":[{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all three PRs. Let me analyze each:\n\n**#55153 - fix: preserve valid Kimi tool call arguments in malformed-args repair**\n- PR, 2 files changed, +62/-1\n- A very clean bug fix with a regression test\n- Clear scope: only Kimi provider, only the malformed-args repair wrapper\n- Already has regression test, CI likely green\n- Linked to #54442\n- Very well documented PR description\n- Assessment: This is a low-risk, well-scoped fix. Review opportunity is minimal because it's a small, isolated change with a clear test. The PR author has been thorough.\n\n\n- Recommendation: Low priority to review since it's well-scoped and likely passing CI.\n\n**#55143 - Bug: acp-router matches internal role dispatch as external ACP intent**\n- Issue, not PR\n- No minimal repro yet\n- The issue describes a real problem in mixed internal-role + optional-ACP setups\n- The workaround (local hard boundary) exists, but the actual fix hasn't been implemented yet\n- Assessment: This is a genuine bug, but it's complex, lacks a minimal repro, and the proposed fix needs more definition. This is better suited as a reference for understanding the problem rather than something to directly review.\n- The reporter could potentially convert their workaround into a PR, though that would require verification of scope.\n\n**#55137 - ACP: sanitize terminal tool titles**\n- PR, 6 files changed, +50/-6\n- Focuses on sanitizing ACP tool titles and ANSI escape sequences in terminal displays\n- Good test coverage and validation\n- Clean scope\n- The risk is relatively low—mostly dealing with display sanitization and ANSI handling.\n\nNow I'm thinking about what review value each of these offers. For #55153, I could verify the fix itself and whether the test is sufficient. For #55143, I could validate the diagnosis and see if the proposed fix is the right approach, though the scope isn't fully defined yet. For #55137, I could examine whether the test coverage is adequate and if the ANSI stripping handles edge cases properly. The first one seems most actionable as a review opportunity.\n\nLooking at the actual fix in #55153, the logic adds a guard before clearing arguments—checking that the accumulated text isn't already a valid JSON object before attempting repair. But I'm noticing something odd in the PR description: it mentions both \"add a regression test\" and \"add a regression test for the split-delta completion case,\" which seems redundant or possibly unclear about what's actually being tested.\n\nThe #55143 issue is much harder to tackle without a minimal reproduction—while the diagnosis of acp-router over-triggering feels valid, the proposed fix of \"hard boundaries\" is too vague without concrete details. I could try to construct a test case using internal role dispatch patterns, but without understanding what acp-router is actually matching on, I'd just be guessing. #55137 has actual code I can review for ANSI handling correctness and edge cases, which is more tractable than the others.\n\nMy priority order is #55153 first since it's concrete and ready to assess, then #55137 where I can do real code review, and finally #55143 since it's valuable but needs more ground truth first."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_7xopelllymzc_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"gh api repos/openclaw/openclaw/pulls/55153/reviews --jq '.[].body' 2>/dev/null | head -5 && echo '---' && gh api repos/openclaw/openclaw/pulls/55137/reviews --jq '.[].body' 2>/dev/null | head -5","workdir":"/root/.openclaw/workspaces/workspace-xixi","yieldMs":10000,"timeout":15}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774528915501},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No reviews yet on either PR. Let me check the actual code for #55153 to understand the fix better."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_mf95k1vfrelx_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"gh api repos/openclaw/openclaw/pulls/55153/files --jq '.[].patch' 2>/dev/null","workdir":"/root/.openclaw/workspaces/workspace-xixi","yieldMs":10000,"timeout":15}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774528944817},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the full code patch for #55153. Let me analyze it:\n\nThe fix adds a `isValidJsonObject()` guard function and modifies the else branch of the malformed args repair. When `isValidJsonObject(nextPartialJson)` is true, it skips the destructive clear path.\n\nHowever, I notice a potential issue: the guard checks `isValidJsonObject(nextPartialJson)` but `nextPartialJson` is the accumulated text, not necessarily the final arguments. The guard checks `parsed !== null && typeof parsed === \"object\" && !Array.isArray(parsed)`. This seems correct - it's checking if the accumulated text is already a valid JSON object.\n\nBut wait - the guard is `if (!isValidJsonObject(nextPartialJson))` - so if the accumulated text IS a valid JSON object, it does NOT clear. \n\nThat's the intended behavior. I'm spotting two real concerns though: the `isValidJsonObject` check could produce false positives if intermediate deltas form valid JSON objects before streaming completes, though in this specific case with `path` starting as `\"path\":\"/tmp/report.txt` (invalid) and then becoming valid after closing braces, the logic should work. There's also a naming issue where `isValidJsonObject` checks for JSON objects but the surrounding logic is parsing tool call arguments.\n\nLooking at the test case, the fix correctly preserves parsed arguments when a closing delta completes valid JSON—the first delta produces invalid JSON while the second completes it to a valid object, and the guard properly skips the destructive clear path in that scenario. The implementation appears sound with a clean regression test covering this exact edge case.\n\nThe main limitation is that this hasn't been validated against an actual Kimi session, but that's more of a verification gap than a code problem. My recommendation is to move forward with this assessment."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_qbz43gfi36mj_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"gh api repos/openclaw/openclaw/pulls/55137/files --jq '.[].patch' 2>/dev/null | head -120","workdir":"/root/.openclaw/workspaces/workspace-xixi","yieldMs":10000,"timeout":15}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774528951353},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the full patch for #55137. Let me analyze:\n\nThe PR changes:\n1. `src/terminal/ansi.ts`: Expands ANSI regex from just SGR (`\\x1b[[0-9;]*m`) to full CSI patterns (`\\x1b[[\\x20-\\x3f]*[\\x40-\\x7e]`), which now covers cursor movement (`\\x1b[2K` = clear line, `\\x1b[1A` = cursor up) sequences.\n2. `src/terminal/safe-text.ts`: Adds a regression test for `sanitizeTerminalText` to strip cursor/erase ANSI sequences.\n\n\n3. `src/acp/event-mapper.ts`: Applies `escapeInlineControlChars` to formatted tool titles, sanitizing ANSI from the source before use in permission requests.\n4. `src/acp/client.ts`: Sanitizes `toolCall.title` with `sanitizeTerminalText` in `resolvePermissionRequest`.\n5. Test coverage added across multiple files including new test cases for ANSI stripping and tool title sanitization.\n\nThe approach is sound—expanding the CSI regex to catch cursor and erase sequences prevents terminal UI manipulation—but there's a potential inconsistency: `formatToolTitle` uses `escapeInlineControlChars` while `resolvePermissionRequest` uses `sanitizeTerminalText`, and I need to verify these are actually equivalent in their sanitization behavior.\n\nLooking at what each function does, `escapeInlineControlChars` converts unprintable characters to hex representations like `\\x1b[2K`, whereas `sanitizeTerminalText` appears to strip them entirely—so the two paths could produce different outputs for the same input, which seems like it might be unintentional.\n\nThe PR description doesn't really explain the rationale behind choosing different sanitization approaches, but that's probably not critical since the test coverage is solid and the logic is straightforward. The risk is pretty low here—it's display sanitization rather than a core functional change.\n\nFor #55153, the Kimi tool call fix looks solid with solid tests backing it up, though it could use some attention. As for #55143, this one is more of a conceptual issue about acp-router misinterpreting internal role dispatch patterns, but without a minimal reproduction and with the proposed fix still vague, it's hard to move forward without more concrete details.\n\nSo my priority order is #55153 first since it's the most concrete and well-bounded, then #55137 where I can spot the actual code review issues, and finally #55143 which needs either a minimal repro or a clearer fix proposal from the maintainers."},{"type":"text","text":"好，直接回 main。\n\n---\n\n**状态：成功**\n**任务：调研 #55153 / #55143 / #55137，评估验证价值并排序**\n\n---\n\n**优先级排序：**\n\n**#1 — #55153（推荐优先 review）**\n推荐动作：`confirm fix + leave practical review comment`\n理由：代码 patch 干净，regression test 精确（streaming delta 分两段 `}` 完成 valid JSON），逻辑正确。但 PR 说明里有一处不精确：`isValidJsonObject()` 实际检查的是\"accumulated text 是否已是 valid JSON object\"，而不是\"final arguments 是否 valid\"——这个区别值得在 review 里提一句，帮助 maintainer 确认边界理解一致。另外 PR 自述\"未做 live Kimi session 验证\"，如实但也说明这是唯一缺口。有实际审过价值的 PR。\n\n**#2 — #55137（推荐 review）**\n推荐动作：`code review`\n理由：ANSI stripping 扩张从 SGR-only 到 full CSI，覆盖了 cursor movement（`\\x1b[2K`）/erase/`\\x1b[1A` 等序列，逻辑方向对，test coverage 到位。但存在一个潜在不一致：`formatToolTitle` 用 `escapeInlineControlChars`（转换为 `\\x1b[2K` 形式），`resolvePermissionRequest` 用 `sanitizeTerminalText`（直接 strip）。两者对同一输入可能产生不同输出——这个值得在 review 里确认是否是预期行为。另外 `ANSI_CSI_PATTERN` 覆盖了 ESC [ 20-3F 40-7E，这个范围较宽，需确认是否有可能误杀合法输出。\n\n**#3 — #55143（推荐作参考，暂不直接 review）**\n推荐动作：`confirm understanding + offer concrete follow-up`\n理由：issue 描述的\"acp-router 在混合 internal-role + 外部 ACP 设置里过度触发\"是真实场景痛点，有